whether armor class should be fixed or monotonically dependent or a
random function of your armor class rating seems to be at issue now.

the fixed armor class (no difference between rating and effective armor
class) is simple but leads to "invincibility" as your AC rating gets
better. the monsters simply can't hit you. that's too easy and no fun!

the monotonically dependent effective armor class scheme seeks to
remedy this by applying a monotonic function to your high (low) ended
armor class so that it is no longer linearly dependent. to the player
unaware of the functional dependence of armor class this means that
he/she must try a little harder but can still become invincible. the
monsters eventually simply can't hit you. see above ...

the randomly effective armor class scheme addresses the invincibilty
issue by generating an armor class that ranges from what you'd like to
believe you have (your AC rating) to one that is attackable on an
attack by attack basis. to the player this means that occasionally no
matter how hard you try a monster may hit you. you're not ever
invincible. so you must be clever to survive. this is challenging,
frustrating, outrageous and the way it should be. leaning on your AC
rating is a normal desire that all players share. however, AC is a
feeble crutch. after about AC= -5 the magic afforded by your choice
of armor becomes more important than the raw AC rating itself.

i wish that:
you could dip armor/weapons into the blessed carcasses of corrosive
monsters and thereby rustproof the dipped item. the carcass, of course,
magically disappears during this process. if such a thing were possible
oracles would surely know.

